@meta
id: 09-04
title: Change takes a motion too
chapter: 09
type: lesson
skills: op-motion
par: 34
minutes: 4
vscode: double-click the identifier and type over it
open: customer.provider.ts

@brief
The error code in this provider is wrong. The registry calls it
`CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND`; the provider throws `CUSTOMER_MISSING`, so the
handler never matches it and the API returns a 500.

Rename the thrown code to `CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND` and save. The quotes stay
as they are.

@keys
| `cw` | change from the cursor to the end of this word |
| `ce` | the same thing, spelled with a different motion |
| `fC` | forward to the next capital C |

@hints
`d` is not the only operator that takes a motion. Every one of them does,
and the one that deletes and drops you into insert mode is `c`.
--
`cw` deletes the word from the cursor onwards and leaves you typing. An
underscore is a word character, so `CUSTOMER_MISSING` is one word, not
two.
--
`10G` to the `throw` line, `fC` onto the `C` of the code, then `cw`, type
the new code, `Esc`.

@solution
    10G fC cw CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Most of those keystrokes are the new name, which is the sign of an edit
done properly — you paid for the text you wanted and nothing else.

`cw` has one wrinkle worth knowing now rather than being surprised by
later. `dw` deletes up to the start of the next word, space included.
`cw` does not: it behaves like `ce` and stops at the end of the current
word, leaving the space alone. Vim special-cases it, because changing a
word and then having to retype the space that followed it would be
maddening.

That is the only place in the grammar where an operator quietly alters its
motion. Everywhere else, the motion means exactly what it means on its
own — which is why `cw` on whitespace, or in the middle of a word, does
what the plain `w` would have told you it does.

Two habits follow. Use `ce` if you want to stop guessing which rule
applies; it is unambiguous. And use `f` to land on the thing you are about
to operate on, rather than counting `w` presses past dots and brackets.

@start customer.provider.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';

@injectable()
export class CustomerProvider {
  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const customer = await this.customerRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!customer) {
      throw exception('CUSTOMER_MISSING');
    }

    return customer;
  }
}

@expected customer.provider.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';

@injectable()
export class CustomerProvider {
  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const customer = await this.customerRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!customer) {
      throw exception('CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return customer;
  }
}
